1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,133
A spinach leaf is green.

2
00:00:02,133 --> 00:00:05,067
A ripe mango leaf in
the sun is shiny green.

3
00:00:05,067 --> 00:00:07,992
And an old leaf turns
yellow before it falls.

4
00:00:08,392 --> 00:00:10,442
Green is not just one colour.

5
00:00:10,442 --> 00:00:13,149
Several colours are hiding inside a leaf.

6
00:00:13,599 --> 00:00:17,208
Aarav crushed a leaf and dotted
the juice on a strip of paper.

7
00:00:17,208 --> 00:00:19,104
He dipped the end in a liquid.

8
00:00:19,104 --> 00:00:22,118
He asked: why did the green
spot split into bands?

9
00:00:22,118 --> 00:00:24,285
And where in the leaf is food made?

10
00:00:24,635 --> 00:00:26,188
Here is the short answer.

11
00:00:26,188 --> 00:00:29,735
A leaf has four pigments, and
they travel at different speeds.

12
00:00:29,735 --> 00:00:33,144
And food is made inside tiny
green bodies called chloroplasts.

13
00:00:33,644 --> 00:00:37,962
By the end, you will know where
photosynthesis happens, the parts of a

14
00:00:37,962 --> 00:00:41,766
chloroplast, the four pigments,
and the two spectrum graphs.

15
00:00:42,416 --> 00:00:44,830
First, where does photosynthesis happen?

16
00:00:45,180 --> 00:00:49,292
It happens in the green parts of
a plant, mostly in the leaves.

17
00:00:49,292 --> 00:00:51,127
Green stems can do it too.

18
00:00:51,577 --> 00:00:55,353
Inside a leaf, the cells packed with
chloroplasts are the mesophyll cells.

19
00:00:55,353 --> 00:00:59,065
The chloroplasts line up along the
cell walls, so they get the best amount

20
00:00:59,065 --> 00:00:59,965
of light.

21
00:01:00,364 --> 00:01:02,006
Let's zoom into a leaf.

22
00:01:02,356 --> 00:01:06,043
Inside the leaf are mesophyll
cells, full of green chloroplasts.

23
00:01:06,543 --> 00:01:08,439
Zoom into one chloroplast.

24
00:01:08,439 --> 00:01:13,505
It has a membrane system: stacks called
grana, joined by flat sheets called

25
00:01:13,505 --> 00:01:14,745
stroma lamellae.

26
00:01:14,745 --> 00:01:17,415
Around them is a fluid, the stroma.

27
00:01:17,915 --> 00:01:19,143
Zoom into one granum.

28
00:01:19,143 --> 00:01:21,606
It is a stack of flat
discs called thylakoids.

29
00:01:21,606 --> 00:01:23,893
The pigments sit in the
thylakoid membranes.

30
00:01:24,543 --> 00:01:27,530
Now let's separate the
pigments, step by step.

31
00:01:27,530 --> 00:01:30,206
This method is called
paper chromatography.

32
00:01:30,556 --> 00:01:31,456
Step one.

33
00:01:31,296 --> 00:01:34,886
Put a dot of leaf extract near
the bottom of a paper strip.

34
00:01:35,636 --> 00:01:36,536
Step two.

35
00:01:36,389 --> 00:01:40,602
Dip the bottom edge in a solvent,
a liquid that climbs up the paper.

36
00:01:41,352 --> 00:01:42,252
Step three.

37
00:01:42,123 --> 00:01:44,142
The solvent carries the pigments up.

38
00:01:44,142 --> 00:01:46,212
Each pigment moves at its own speed.

39
00:01:46,962 --> 00:01:47,862
Step four.

40
00:01:47,796 --> 00:01:49,071
Four bands appear.

41
00:01:49,071 --> 00:01:51,534
Chlorophyll a is bright or blue green.

42
00:01:51,534 --> 00:01:53,500
Chlorophyll b is yellow green.

43
00:01:54,250 --> 00:01:55,150
Step five.

44
00:01:54,867 --> 00:01:56,024
Xanthophylls are yellow.

45
00:01:56,024 --> 00:01:57,876
Carotenoids are yellow to yellow orange.

46
00:01:58,626 --> 00:01:59,595
Remember this.

47
00:01:59,595 --> 00:02:01,360
Leaf green is four pigments.

48
00:02:01,960 --> 00:02:04,059
Now, which one does the main job?

49
00:02:04,059 --> 00:02:04,987
Chlorophyll a.

50
00:02:04,987 --> 00:02:06,505
It is the chief pigment.

51
00:02:06,855 --> 00:02:09,624
The other three are
called accessory pigments.

52
00:02:09,624 --> 00:02:11,145
Accessory means helping.

53
00:02:11,145 --> 00:02:15,462
They absorb other colours of light
and pass the energy to chlorophyll a.

54
00:02:15,912 --> 00:02:20,029
They also protect chlorophyll a from
photo-oxidation, which is damage by

55
00:02:20,029 --> 00:02:21,144
too much light.

56
00:02:21,794 --> 00:02:23,605
Scientists draw two graphs.

57
00:02:23,605 --> 00:02:25,219
Here is the difference.

58
00:02:25,569 --> 00:02:29,244
The absorption spectrum shows how much
light of each colour a pigment absorbs.

59
00:02:29,244 --> 00:02:31,928
Chlorophyll a absorbs most
in the blue and red regions.

60
00:02:32,378 --> 00:02:36,834
The action spectrum shows how much
photosynthesis happens in each colour.

61
00:02:36,834 --> 00:02:38,954
It also peaks in blue and red.

62
00:02:38,954 --> 00:02:40,908
The two graphs match closely.

63
00:02:41,558 --> 00:02:45,030
There is a clear division of
labour inside the chloroplast.

64
00:02:45,380 --> 00:02:48,416
The membrane system traps light energy
and makes two energy carriers, ATP

65
00:02:48,416 --> 00:02:49,316
and NADPH.

66
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:50,377
These are the light reactions.

67
00:02:50,827 --> 00:02:54,659
In the stroma, enzymes use them to
make sugar, which then forms starch.

68
00:02:54,659 --> 00:02:57,938
These are the carbon reactions,
sometimes called dark reactions.

69
00:02:58,538 --> 00:02:59,438
Your turn.

70
00:02:59,290 --> 00:03:03,330
Chlorophyll a absorbs the most light
in which colours: green, or blue and

71
00:03:03,330 --> 00:03:04,230
red?

72
00:03:03,771 --> 00:03:04,876
Pause and decide.

73
00:03:08,076 --> 00:03:09,062
Blue and red!

74
00:03:09,062 --> 00:03:11,537
That is where most photosynthesis happens.

75
00:03:12,187 --> 00:03:13,768
Now, a worked example.

76
00:03:13,768 --> 00:03:16,882
Read one chromatography
strip, from top to bottom.

77
00:03:17,232 --> 00:03:18,973
The top band is yellow to orange.

78
00:03:18,973 --> 00:03:20,241
That is the carotenoids.

79
00:03:20,241 --> 00:03:22,588
Below it, the yellow
band is the xanthophylls.

80
00:03:23,038 --> 00:03:26,048
Next comes the blue green
band, chlorophyll a.

81
00:03:26,048 --> 00:03:28,808
Then the yellow green band, chlorophyll b.

82
00:03:29,258 --> 00:03:31,339
So, chief pigment: chlorophyll a.

83
00:03:31,339 --> 00:03:33,464
Accessory pigments: the other three.

84
00:03:34,114 --> 00:03:35,575
Now, the exam trap.

85
00:03:35,925 --> 00:03:38,201
Many students think dark
reactions happen only at night.

86
00:03:38,601 --> 00:03:40,499
But the name does not mean darkness.

87
00:03:40,499 --> 00:03:43,943
These reactions use ATP and NADPH
from the light reactions, so they run

88
00:03:43,943 --> 00:03:44,849
in daytime too.

89
00:03:45,499 --> 00:03:46,838
Where is this asked?

90
00:03:46,838 --> 00:03:51,116
As statement questions on pigments,
accessory pigments and the two spectra.

91
00:03:51,716 --> 00:03:52,866
Exam level now.

92
00:03:52,866 --> 00:03:54,563
Read the four statements.

93
00:03:54,563 --> 00:03:55,762
Pause and solve.

94
00:04:01,162 --> 00:04:02,381
The answer is D.

95
00:04:02,381 --> 00:04:05,141
Statements one, three
and four are correct.

96
00:04:05,641 --> 00:04:07,922
Chlorophyll a is the chief pigment.

97
00:04:07,922 --> 00:04:12,079
Accessory pigments protect it from
photo-oxidation, and the stroma makes

98
00:04:12,079 --> 00:04:12,979
sugar.

99
00:04:13,169 --> 00:04:15,902
But chlorophyll b is
yellow green, not yellow.

100
00:04:15,902 --> 00:04:17,326
Xanthophylls are yellow.

101
00:04:17,326 --> 00:04:18,942
So statement two is wrong.

102
00:04:19,542 --> 00:04:21,082
Here is your recap.

103
00:04:21,082 --> 00:04:22,665
Screenshot this card.

104
00:04:25,865 --> 00:04:28,050
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

105
00:04:28,450 --> 00:04:29,350
One.

106
00:04:28,852 --> 00:04:30,309
What are grana and stroma?

107
00:04:31,209 --> 00:04:32,109
Two.

108
00:04:31,622 --> 00:04:33,200
Name the four leaf pigments.

109
00:04:34,100 --> 00:04:35,000
Three.

110
00:04:34,645 --> 00:04:38,264
How is an action spectrum different
from an absorption spectrum?

111
00:04:39,364 --> 00:04:44,093
Next time: how does a flash of light
knock an electron loose and send it

112
00:04:44,093 --> 00:04:45,669
down a zig zag path?

113
00:04:45,669 --> 00:04:49,431
We find out in the light
reaction and electron transport.
